# Remediation
Fixed in commit `fd74f68` (`Validate multipart part number bounds consistently`).

The fix restores explicit `InvalidArgument` validation for multipart part
numbers at the API entry points and also adds a shared bounds check in
`create_upload_part_stream_session`, so both plain `UploadPart` and
`UploadPartCopy` enforce the AWS `1..=10000` limit consistently.

Regression coverage was added for:
- server-core `UploadPartCopy` invalid part number
- external `s3-tests` plain `UploadPart` invalid part number
- external `s3-tests` `UploadPartCopy` invalid part number

# Summary
The commit introduces a regression where UploadPartCopy no longer enforces the multipart part-number upper bound, allowing oversized part numbers to be stored.
Previously, UploadPartCopy used begin_stream_part, which validates part_number is within 1..=10,000. The refactor bypasses that path and calls create_upload_part_stream_session directly. The new authorize_upload_part_copy path only feeds part_number into the SSE-C scope helper (which just enforces nonzero/u16), so values above 10,000 are now accepted. This lets authenticated users create parts beyond the intended limit, violating S3 semantics and increasing metadata/disk usage (availability impact).

